    {
      "title": "Google Maps satellite: owner-supplied local reference",
      "url": "https://www.google.com/maps/@13.6466754,123.2368358,80m/data=!3m1!1e3",
      "note": "Inspected owner-supplied naga-city-sports-complex.png on 2026-10-08; capture date unknown. URL locates the site. Extensive perimeter crown belt around oval, west stand-side canopy, north court/pool-side crowns and southeast edge grove; green verges are bounded separately from track, court aprons and tan infield. No distinguishable low hedge or formal bed can be separated from the large contiguous crowns; none inferred. Mapped additional north court remains even where absent from this older image. Canopy outside the sports envelope or in river/farmland is recorded, not added to another territory. Crown-width observations: Large north/west/south clumps about 90-110 px; eastern oval-edge crowns about 60-85 px; pool/court gaps smaller. Image roof sections matched separately to complete source roof corners; excluded opposite corners on distinct roofs provide independent checks. Mapped older roof sizes differ from imagery; sections are used independently rather than smoothing incompatible dimensions across the yard. Checks establish local roof-section accuracy, not a surveyed campus-wide transformation. Visible greenery is authored relative to its nearest matched section. Independent excluded check errors: 0.00 m, 0.21 m, 0.01 m, 0.33 m. Visible crown centers are fitted to clear trunks within the observed crown footprint (maximum seven metres); crowns may overhang roofs and roads. Crown widths in the dense belts are estimated from image pixels against their matched local roof scale; existing circular crowns approximate irregular/elliptical image crowns. Complete mapped roofs, pitches, full-width paths/roads, water and farmland remain unchanged. Planted islands take precedence over exposed paving; ground under opaque canopy is not invented. No surveyed boundary, species, construction date or exact inventory inferred; no bitmap ships."
    }
  ]
